this vast landscape is just part of what  many call  malwapu the land of the indigenous  mapuche  a rich fertile region stretching south  of the biobio river for 400 kilometers  today it's part of chile but as these  mapuche flags indicate  some of the land is being reclaimed  increasingly by force  in the last four months there have been  nearly daily arson attacks on trains  trucks and forestry companies that now  own the lion's share of the land  but also against farms and homes owned  by ordinary chileans  and people on both sides of the conflict  are being killed  outside of the jail in angol mapucha  community leaders announced  they'll fight by any means necessary for  the release of those they call  political prisoners of chile accused of  carrying out  attacks we are being persecuted and  imprisoned for demanding our land  for many years we were repressed but now  we've learned to organise and defend  ourselves  spokesman says new deployments of  chilean special forces units to the  region  are escalating the confrontation it's  getting dark here and  we're going we're starting to see now  military trucks  passing by this is what the local media  calls  the gaza strip and we're also seeing  here trees that have been chopped down  to try to block the road  so the cars can't pass and it will get  worse as  the night goes on as it becomes darker  because it's part of an ongoing campaign  of sabotage on the part of those who say  that they have to expel  the people who have usurped their land  for hundreds of years  neither the spaniards nor the subsequent  chilean state  was able to conquer the combat of  mapuche but by 1880  chile's army succeeded in driving them  off most of their land  destroying their rural economy and  plunging them into deep poverty that  persist  to this day  [Music]  now many communities are trying to take  back what they lost  like this property owned by chile's  largest forestry company  but they say it's also about countering  140 years of humiliation  today they've gathered for a game of  palin an ancestral sport played between  two communities in order to reinforce  their ethnic  and cultural bonds thirty-two-year-old  susanna soto says that like many  mapuches  her grandparents took up spanish names  to try to offset  deep racism and discrimination by  chileans  some of us may not have mapuche names  anymore but we still have mapuche blood  and we aren't ashamed of it we are from  a new generation that has the strength  to rise up again  [Music]  more than 12 percent of chileans  identify as mapuche  yet chile's constitution does not  recognize their existence as indigenous  people  supermarket owner delfin size says he  too belongs here  he takes us to see the remains of his  family's country home  just hours after it was burnt to the  ground by unidentified armed men  i feel nostalgia anger knowing they  burned down something that belonged to  us nearly 100 years  we can't rebuild our memories but i'm  going to rebuild the house  right here because no one can throw us  off our land  those resorting to violence are a  minority  but miguel angel says they're part of a  larger ancestral conflict that the  chilean state is refusing to address  except with force that's a military  plane with state-of-the-art technology  to intercept  telephone and radio signals it overflies  day and night when you see things like  this who can deny that this territory is  being militarized  impossible what's also become obvious  is that there's no easy solution to a  complex dispute  to which most chileans were oblivious  until now  that it's become impossible to ignore  lucia newman al jazeera  peleca chile
 

